<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lago Oil and Transport Company</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1924">1924</a>–<a href="/years/1985">1985</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lago Oil and Transport Company ran a large oil refinery at San Nicolaas in southern Aruba from 1929 to 1985. Owned by Standard Oil of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-jersey">New Jersey</a>, later Exxon, it processed Venezuelan crude and was for a time among the largest refineries in the world, drawing workers from across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>. Its closure in 1985 cost Aruba much of its income and pushed the island towards tourism.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lago-oil-and-transport-company.jpg" alt="Lago Oil and Transport Company" width="320" height="307" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Willem van de Poll · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:De_Lago-olieraffinaderij_in_San_Nicolas_op_Aruba,_Bestanddeelnr_252-8040.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Large oil reserves had been found beneath <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-maracaibo">Lake Maracaibo</a> in Venezuela, but the channel into the lake was too shallow for ocean-going tankers. The Pan American Petroleum and Transport Company, which held a lease on much of the lake, founded the Lago Petroleum Corporation to run lake tankers carrying the crude to Aruba for transshipment. On 14 August 1924 its agent, the Scottish captain Robert Rodgers, surveyed the harbour at San Nicolas, where the wharf and railway of the former Aruba Phosphate Company stood, and by November 1924 work on the harbour and an oil depot was under way. Construction of a refinery began on 24 February 1928.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1928 Standard Oil of Indiana bought the Aruba facility and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-maracaibo">Lake Maracaibo</a> holdings and began a small refinery beside the port. Expecting a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> tax on imported crude, it sold everything to Standard Oil of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-jersey">New Jersey</a>, which had markets around the world. In 1938 that company won a contract to supply <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> with 100 octane aviation gasoline on condition that it be made outside the United States, and the refinery on Aruba, then under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> control, was enlarged to meet it.</p><p>Wartime demand brought further expansion, and Lago became one of the largest refineries in the world, second only to the Royal Dutch Shell refinery on Curaçao. On 16 February 1942 the German submarine U-156 attacked the site, but its deck gun exploded through the gunners' mistakes and the refinery was not damaged, although three of the lake tankers were torpedoed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In the years after the war the refinery ran at full capacity with more than 10,000 employees. About a thousand were foreign staff in supervisory posts, who lived in a company town called Lago Colony east of the refinery; the rest were people of Aruba and workers brought in mainly from the British West Indies, who lived in San Nicolaas and the interior. Exxon closed the refinery on 31 March 1985 and began to dismantle it and the colony. Before it was fully dismantled, the Aruba Government took it over and sold it to the Coastal Corporation, which reopened it well below its former capacity; it later passed to Valero Energy Corporation and, late in 2017, to Citgo Petroleum Corporation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lago Oil and Transport Company” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The closure of the <b>Lago</b> oil refinery in 1985 forced a rapid turn to tourism, which now dominates.</blockquote><a href="/state/aruba">Aruba</a></li></ul></section>
